            <ArticleTitle>The physiological and pathophysiological roles of adipocyte miRNAs.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>195-202</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1139/bcb-2012-0053</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>MicroRNAs (miRNAs) are highly conserved, small, noncoding RNAs that regulate gene expression at the posttranscriptional level. Their actions affect numerous important biological processes, including adipocyte differentiation and function, sugar and lipid metabolism, and insulin production and secretion. Recent reports suggest miRNAs may also be involved in the pathogenic processes of obesity, diabetes, and insulin resistance. In this review, we summarize research progresses on adipocyte miRNAs and their physiological and pathological implications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
